Date: Tue, 13 Jul 93 16:23:22 EDT
From: eisrael@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: NANO: A TM-5 the size of a red blood cell?

With some of the list's most prominent members continuing to bicker
about who's more obnoxious than whom, it falls to the lesser lights to
try to spark truly extropian conversations...

I've been reading _Nanosystems_ and _Unbounding the Future_ lately,
concentrating on the sections regarding nano-scale computational
devices. Frankly, the ideas are downright amazing. However, while the
idea of a cubical computer only a few hundred nanometers on a side
that is capable of 1 quadrillion instructions per second (1 X 10^17)
is stunning, _Nanosystems_ leaves out any discussion of the peripherals
that would inevitably be required for use with this nanoCPU, saying
only that their design doesn't seem to present any problems.

Just for a minute, though, let's think about a machine that can perform
a quadrillion instructions per second. To get a handle on the numbers,
I worked it out: Say my modern workstation can process 100 million
instructions per second. That's 1 X 10^8. Meaning that in order to
process the same number of instructions that the nanoCPU handles
IN ONE SECOND, my desktop workstation would have to work continuously
for 10^9 (one billion) seconds, or just under 32 years!

(I say that it "processes the same number of instructions" rather than
"does the same amount of work" because the former is more precise than
the latter, and also because _Nanosystems_ doesn't specify what kinds
of instruction sets might be amenable or unworkable for a nanoCPU, which
leaves open the possibility that a nanoCPU instruction set might be
quite different from that of a macroCPU.)

All of which opens up a host of questions. How do you design the
peripherals for this nanoCPU? Can they keep up with this phenomenal
processing rate?  What does a data bus look like in the nano-world? Can
nanoCPUs be inter-connected to create cell-sized parallel-processing
architectures? Is the design and development of parts in 3-space
required by the pushrod-style nanoCPU significantly more difficult than
the layered but still largely 2-D process of developing chip masks?

In addition, the idea of machines this powerful floating through living
tissue is reason to give any software engineer pause: though much work
has been done, how can we ever raise the art of software development to
the point where we'd trust the code to control machines with such
immense destructive power?

What other doors does this technology open? Neural interfaces to global
information search-and-retrieval systems? Automatic, self-configuring
replacement neurons?

As the Chrysler company likes to say, "this changes everything." How
are we going to adapt to computers too powerful to comprehend and too
numerous to count?

Elias Israel
