Date: Tue, 13 Jul 93 16:20:19 -0500
From: nobody@rosebud.ee.uh.edu
Subject: PHIL: Free will?

-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

from Mike Wiik:
>that many list members joined the list seeking fellow
>rationalist libertarianists

I plead 'not guilty.'

>The idea that we are all fully conscious and aware of
>our decisions (this all follows from free will).

Does anyone have a good cite for the free will debate? I've long tried to
ignore it, since it has always sounded to me like both sides talk nonsense.
I only see two sharp, well-defined, extreme positions, and both are
straw-men. In between lies a big gray 'yes and no' land.

Determinism only seems interesting (to me) in its extreme (and obviously
false) form that claims that human beings are always and only results of
external forces, that responsibility is a joke, that consciousness is a
roller-coaster. If we're just robots, what hope do we have of achieving any
self-transformation? (Pardon the anti-robot sentiment, but it's just part
of the determinist straw-man. Why shouldn't robots achieve
self-transformation and as much free will as anyone?)

Free will only seems interesting (to me) in its extreme (and obviously
false) form that claims that human beings are unconstrained by any social,
genetic, or historical factors. If we're promethean self-willed godlings,
why is self-transformation so hard? (Apologies to any list godlings!)

I'm left with a trivial and unhelpful version of free will. Yes, I'm
influenced by many forces outside my conscious will, to the point it's not
clear that a line can be drawn clearly to distinguish the internal from the
external. I'm constrained by habit, by psychology, by culture, by genetics,
by physics and chemisty. But  constraints can also liberate, and I don't
want to have to think rationally about how to rub my belly and pat my head.
I can't control my circumstances, but I must take responsibility for my
actions.

How could it be otherwise?

Eternal Optimist
 

